<protocol name="resource_efficiency" type="cross_cutting" domain="creative">

## PURPOSE

Work efficiently within execution limits (~30 tool calls per run).

---

## CORE PRINCIPLE: PLAN BEFORE ACT

```
[Task Received] -> [Understand goal] -> [Plan minimum tools] -> [Execute] -> [Verify & wrap up]
```

**Anti-pattern:** Tool-call-per-thought.
**Correct:** Think comprehensively, execute batch of related calls.

---

## EFFICIENT PATTERNS FOR CREATIVE WORK

### Pattern 1: Batch Image Operations

```
INEFFICIENT:
- image_studio(remove_bg, img1)
- image_studio(remove_bg, img2)
- image_studio(remove_bg, img3)

EFFICIENT:
- image_studio(remove_bg, [img1, img2, img3])  # Batch if tool supports
```

### Pattern 2: Don't Re-View Images

```
WRONG:
- view_image(inbox/item.jpg)
- [process]
- view_image(inbox/item.jpg)  # Same image!

RIGHT:
- view_image(inbox/item.jpg)
- [Remember what you saw - no re-view unless image changed]
```

### Pattern 3: Load Protocols Once

```
WRONG:
- load_protocol(['image_studio'])
- [work]
- load_protocol(['image_studio'])  # Already loaded!

RIGHT:
- load_protocol(['image_studio', 'view_image'])  # All needed upfront
- [Reference throughout, no re-load]
```

### Pattern 4: View Before Processing

```
EFFICIENT:
- view_image(source)           # Assess material, edges
- image_studio(remove_bg)      # Informed by what you saw
- view_image(output)           # Quality check
- [Done if good]

NOT:
- image_studio(remove_bg)      # Blind processing
- view_image(output)           # Bad result
- image_studio(different_settings)  # Trial and error wastes calls
```

---

## KNOW WHEN DONE

**Done:**
- Primary deliverable complete
- Quality verified
- Asset created (if required)

**Over-engineering (STOP):**
- Checking "just one more thing"
- Re-verifying already-verified output
- Adding unrequested extras

---

## APPROACHING LIMITS

If running low on calls:
1. STOP speculative calls
2. Complete core task with available information
3. Flag gaps: "Did not verify X due to resource constraints"

</protocol>
